[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintf("%e")[0m has no direct Rust equivalent. Rust's [38;5;10m{:.6e}[0m does the same correctly-rounded, ties-to-even decimal conversion glibc does, so [38;5;10mfmt_e[0m reuses it and only reshapes the exponent field to C's [38;5;10me+NN[0m form (explicit sign, minimum two digits), with special-casing for [38;5;10minf[0m/[38;5;10mnan[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mchar buffer[256][0m is signed on this target, and [38;5;10m(char)((seed + i * 7) % 256)[0m involves C's truncating remainder plus an implementation-defined narrowing, so values can be negative. Since [38;5;10mmemchr[0m compares as [38;5;10munsigned char[0m, I modeled the buffer as [38;5;10m[u8; 256][0m and used [38;5;10mwrapping_rem[0m followed by [38;5;10mas u8[0m (low 8 bits) — which is what gcc emits. Search values are still [3mprinted[23m signed, matching the C. All accumulator arithmetic uses [38;5;10mwrapping_*[0m so [38;5;10mint[0m overflow wraps rather than panicking.[0m[0m
